I implemented the song algorithmically by storing the animals and their associated comment lines, building each verse cumulatively, and joining multiple verses with a blank line.

FoodChain.java
```
class FoodChain {

    private static final String[] ANIMALS = {
        "fly",
        "spider",
        "bird",
        "cat",
        "dog",
        "goat",
        "cow",
        "horse"
    };

    private static final String[] COMMENTS = {
        "",
        "It wriggled and jiggled and tickled inside her.",
        "How absurd to swallow a bird!",
        "Imagine that, to swallow a cat!",
        "What a hog, to swallow a dog!",
        "Just opened her throat and swallowed a goat!",
        "I don't know how she swallowed a cow!",
        "She's dead, of course!"
    };

    String verse(int verse) {
        if (verse < 1 || verse > 8) {
            return "";
        }

        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
        sb.append("I know an old lady who swallowed a ")
          .append(ANIMALS[verse - 1])
          .append(".\n");

        if (verse == 8) {
            sb.append(COMMENTS[7]).append("\n");
            return sb.toString();
        }

        if (verse > 1) {
            sb.append(COMMENTS[verse - 1]).append("\n");
        }

        for (int previous = verse - 2; previous >= 0; previous--) {
            String current = ANIMALS[previous + 1];
            String caught = ANIMALS[previous];

            if (previous == 1) {
                caught += " that wriggled and jiggled and tickled inside her.";
            }

            sb.append("She swallowed the ")
              .append(current)
              .append(" to catch the ")
              .append(caught)
              .append(".\n");
        }

        sb.append("I don't know why she swallowed the fly. Perhaps she'll die.\n");
        return sb.toString();
    }

    String verses(int startVerse, int endVerse) {
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();

        for (int i = startVerse; i <= endVerse; i++) {
            if (i > startVerse) {
                sb.append("\n");
            }
            sb.append(verse(i));
        }

        return sb.toString();
    }

}
